{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":87903,"items":[{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7515638","title":["Autonomous Coding Agents for Software Development Testing and Debugging"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-8053-2554","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Yeswanth Kumar","family":"Polishetty","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Autonomous coding agents build on top of large language models to provide more action according to code suggestions: auto code officers look at files, code suggestions, edit, run tools, create tests, debug failures, and iterate over patches. This paper presents an overview of the existing knowledge regarding their application in the development, testing and debugging of software and suggests a guided lifecycle towards their application in production. The analysis differentiates between model capability and system capability in that the ability to retrieve a repository, interfaces of tools, execution environment, a test oracle, memory and approval gates are what would determine whether an agent could come up with a reliable change. Benchmarks have demonstrated high improvement in the initial results of SWE-bench to more interactive and structured repair systems, but the benchmark contamination, weak tests, underspecified problems, cost variance and non-equivalent evaluation environments provide hindrance to the straightforward leaderboard comparisons. It suggests a five-step operating model which includes, specifying the task, localizing the repository, implementation of the task by constrained means, validation through evidence and integration control. The architecture integrates sandboxed, least-privilege credentials, provenance capturing, policy-as-code, independent test generation, human inspection and rollback. Benchmark evidence, lifecycle coverage and autonomy risk tradeoffs, and validation controls are grouped into four figures of analysis and four tables. The research concludes that coding agents prove to be most useful with as a supervised engineering system and not as an unconditioned substitute of the developers. Measuring functional correctness, regression risk, security, maintainability, cost and operational safety is a combination needed to be taken to ensure that adoption is reliable. The research does not claim an original experiment on human subjects or production and explicit figures of the models are mentioned.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7515638","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T11:15:37Z","timestamp":1790248537000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3837729.3850161","title":["In the Age of AI Coding Agents, Keeping the Engineer in Software Engineering (Keynote)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5178-3496","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Elena","family":"Glassman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Harvard University, Cambridge, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3837729.3850161","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,3]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T22:58:26Z","timestamp":1790895506000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834471","title":["Debug2Fix: Can Interactive Debugging Help Coding Agents Fix More Bugs?"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-0140-2785","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Spandan","family":"Garg","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Microsoft Corporation, Redmond, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1665-1875","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yufan","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Microsoft Corporation, Redmond, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834471","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834471","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Large language model (LLM) agents integrate external tools with one or more LLMs to accomplish specific tasks. Agents have rapidly been adopted by developers, and they are starting to be deployed in industrial workflows, such as their use to fix static analysis issues from the widely used SonarQube static analyzer. However, the growing importance of agents means their actions carry greater impact and potential risk. Thus, to use them at scale, an additional layer of trust and evidence is necessary. This work presents AutoCodeSherpa, a technique that provides explanations of software issues in the form of symbolic formulae. Inspired by the reachability, infection, and propagation model of software faults, the explanations are composed of input, infection, and output conditions, collectively providing a specification of the issue. In practice, the symbolic explanation is implemented as a combination of a property-based test (PBT) and program-internal symbolic expressions. Critically, this means our symbolic explanations are executable and can be automatically evaluated, unlike natural language explanations. Experiments show the generated conditions are highly accurate. For example, input conditions from AutoCodeSherpa had an accuracy of 85.7%. This high accuracy makes symbolic explanations particularly useful in two scenarios. First, the explanations can be used in automated issue resolution environments to decide whether to accept or reject patches from issue resolution agents; AutoCodeSherpa could reject 2x as many incorrect patches as baselines did. Second, as agentic AI approaches continue to develop, program analysis driven explanations like ours can be provided to other LLM-based repair techniques which do not employ analysis to improve their output. In our experiments, our symbolic explanations could improve the plausible patch generation rate of the Agentless technique by 60%.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3832161","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T19:32:02Z","timestamp":1790883122000},"title":["AutoCodeSherpa: Symbolic Explanations in AI Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0298-5320","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sungmin","family":"Kang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1080-4770","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Haifeng","family":"Ruan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7127-1137","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Abhik","family":"Roychoudhury","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832161","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832161","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3744916.3773250","title":["More with Less: An Empirical Study of Turn-Control Strategies for Efficient Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3800-2565","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pengfei","family":"Gao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2843-0689","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chao","family":"Peng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3744916.3773250","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T12:11:14Z","timestamp":1789128674000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3744916.3773250","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3844178.3844645","title":["From Code to Knowledge: Enhancing Coding Agents via Reverse-Engineered Software Knowledge"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9366-6076","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yiran","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanyang Technological University, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-0957-854X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yifei","family":"Ge","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University, Nanjing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-6662-9020","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaoxia","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3844178.3844645","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:39:43Z","timestamp":1791452383000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3844178.3844645","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Agentic coding tools, such as OpenAI Codex, Claude Code, and Cursor, are changing how software is developed. These AI-powered systems function as autonomous teammates capable of planning and executing complex development tasks. Agents have become active participants in refactoring, a key practice for improving internal code quality without altering observable behavior. Despite their increasing adoption, there is little empirical understanding of how agentic refactoring is used in practice, how it compares to human-driven refactoring, and what impact it has on code quality.<\/jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>To address this empirical gap, we present a large-scale study of AI agent-generated refactorings in real-world open-source Java projects, analyzing 51,412 refactoring instances across 12,256 pull requests and 14,998 commits based on the AIDev dataset. Our empirical analysis shows that refactoring is a common and intentional activity in this development paradigm, with agents explicitly targeting refactoring in 26.1% of commits. The subsequent analyses of refactoring types, purposes, and code quality focus on the 3,907 commits (32,611 instances) with explicit refactoring intent. Analysis of refactoring types reveals that high-level refactorings form the largest category overall. However, agentic refactoring contains a slightly larger share of lowlevel, consistency-oriented edits than human refactoring (27.6% vs. 24.4%), and its two most frequent operations are Rename Variable (7.6%) and Change Variable Type (6.8%). Additionally, the stated motivations behind agentic refactoring focus overwhelmingly on internal quality concerns (with 52.5% in maintainability and 28.1% in readability), although the measured metric improvements from these frequently occurring low-level refactorings remain limited. Furthermore, quantitative evaluation of code quality metrics shows that agentic refactoring yields small but statistically significant improvements in structural metrics, particularly for medium-level changes, reducing class size and complexity.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3859242","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,22]],"date-time":"2026-10-22T00:15:59Z","timestamp":1792628159000},"title":["Agentic Refactoring: An Empirical Study of AI Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-6317-0754","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kosei","family":"Horikawa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4468-5972","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hao","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen\u2019s University, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9633-7577","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yutaro","family":"Kashiwa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7213-4006","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bram","family":"Adams","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen\u2019s University, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2919-6620","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hajimu","family":"Iida","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nara Institute of Science and Technology, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7749-5513","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ahmed E.","family":"Hassan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen\u2019s University, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3859242","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3859242","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,21]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834654","title":["RELY-SE: A Dashboard for Measuring Operational Reliability of AI Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0994-5287","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Akilesh","family":"P","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Tirupati, RISHA Lab, Tirupati, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-1408-5366","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shivadharshan","family":"S","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Tirupati, RISHA Lab, Tirupati, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0818-8178","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sridhar","family":"Chimalakonda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Indian Institute of Technology Tirupati, RISHA Lab, Tirupati, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-2352-2090","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vibhu Saujanya","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture Tech Labs, Bangalore, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9774-6265","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vikrant","family":"Kaulgud","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture Tech Labs, Bangalore, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834654","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834654","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mc.2026.3719093","title":["Artificial Intelligence Coding Agents in Software Engineering Education:: Why Development &amp; Operations and Scale Matter"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-6328-5926","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marko","family":"Mudrinic","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Union University","place":["Belgrade, Serbia"],"department":["School of Computing"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1708-9799","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Milos","family":"Radenkovic","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Union University","place":["Belgrade, Serbia"],"department":["School of Computing"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1874-8602","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sergei P.","family":"Prokhorov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for the History of Science of the Russian Academy of Sciences","place":["Moscow, Russia"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7716-5845","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Aleksandra","family":"Labus","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Belgrade","place":["Belgrade, Serbia"],"department":["Faculty of Organizational Sciences"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2111-7788","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bozidar","family":"Radenkovic","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Belgrade","place":["Belgrade, Serbia"],"department":["Faculty of Organizational Sciences"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"George","family":"Hurlburt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"U.S. Federal Service"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sorel","family":"Reisman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"California State University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mc.2026.3719093","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,24]],"date-time":"2026-09-24T19:04:49Z","timestamp":1790276689000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/2\/11707757\/11707793.pdf?arnumber=11707793","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3844178.3844642","title":["Same Task, Different Fate: Population Structure in Trajectory-Based Analyses of Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-4827-9884","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Simarjot","family":"Khanna","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher, Montreal, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3844178.3844642","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:39:43Z","timestamp":1791452383000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3844178.3844642","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Context<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Conversational Large Language Models (CLLMs) can automatically generate code by collaborating with users through natural language. However, poor collaboration can lead to poor quality output.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Objective<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>This exploratory study aims to investigate how humans and CLLMs can collaborate as peers through vibe coding, an approach that integrates principles from prompt engineering, agile design, and human-AI co-creation to enhance collaboration.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Method<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>We designed four interaction models representing different collaboration patterns in the software development process: the solo model (human-only development), the collaborative model (human\u2013CLLM collaboration), the fully automated model (development autonomously performed by a CLLM), and the agentic model (development autonomously performed by the MetaGPT\u00a0X platform). Based on these models, we implemented corresponding Test-Driven Development (TDD) workflows using structured prompts and Python scripts. We then conducted a controlled pre-experimental study with TDD professionals to compare the solo and collaborative workflows. In addition, we performed repeated exploratory executions of fully automated and agentic workflows on the same development tasks to obtain complementary evidence.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Results<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Our findings suggest that the choice of interaction model should depend on the development objective. Agentic workflows are best suited for rapid development and functionally correct production code but may introduce additional implementation complexity. However, they may also introduce additional implementation decisions that are not explicitly required by the functional specifications, resulting in untested decision points. In contrast, collaborative workflows produce higher-quality, better-organized test suites.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Conclusions<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Our work explored how humans can collaborate with CLLMs in generating code in highly intensive and demanding development process. Our results also show that accurately reproducing development process workflows is yet an open problem, also in the new agentic technologies.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>","DOI":"10.1007\/s10664-026-10950-z","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,9]],"date-time":"2026-10-09T05:07:07Z","timestamp":1791522427000},"title":["Vibe Coding: An experiment with test-driven development"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-3156-6211","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Moritz","family":"Mock","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3737-9264","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Barbara","family":"Russo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s10664-026-10950-z.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/article\/10.1007\/s10664-026-10950-z","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/link.springer.com\/content\/pdf\/10.1007\/s10664-026-10950-z.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10664-026-10950-z","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,9]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3774748.3787755","title":["PAGENT: Learning to Patch Software Engineering Issue Fixing Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-1937-3733","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Haoran","family":"Xue","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"York University, North York, Ontario, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3774748.3787755","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T21:41:26Z","timestamp":1788990086000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3774748.3787755","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843779.3844635","title":["TurnCost: Turns, Not Observations: Cache-Aware Loop Engineering for Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-7426-367X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pramod","family":"Kumar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"The Pennsylvania State University, University Park, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-4553-3018","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Raj Kumar","family":"Pandey","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The Pennsylvania State University, University Park, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843779.3844635","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:33:46Z","timestamp":1791452026000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843779.3844635","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Autonomous coding agents are increasingly participating in collaborative software development, yet little is known about their adoption in functional programming ecosystems. This paper presents an empirical study on the adoption of autonomous coding agents in GitHub repositories developed with functional programming languages. We analyzed 35 repositories written in Elixir, Haskell, F#, Erlang and OCaml, comprising 158 AI-generated Pull Requests. The results show that AI-generated contributions are already present in mature and actively maintained functional programming repositories. Most Pull Requests are small and incremental. OpenAI Codex accounts for 78 of the 93 AI-generated Pull Requests in Elixir repositories, whereas GitHub Copilot is responsible for 38 of the 39 Pull Requests identified in F# repositories. Elixir also presents the highest Pull Request acceptance rate (77.4%), while repository popularity exhibits no meaningful association with AI-generated Pull Request activity (\ud835\udf0c = \u22120.029 for stars and \ud835\udf0c = \u22120.038 for forks).<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.5753\/se4fp.2026.32864","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,23]],"date-time":"2026-10-23T17:12:14Z","timestamp":1792775534000},"title":["Characterizing the Adoption of Autonomous Coding Agents in Functional Programming Repositories"],"author":[{"given":"Kayre","family":"Mendes","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jailton","family":"Coelho","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/sol.sbc.org.br\/index.php\/se4fp\/article\/download\/46180\/45939","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/sol.sbc.org.br\/index.php\/se4fp\/article\/download\/46180\/45939","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5753\/se4fp.2026.32864","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3774748.3787673","title":["Tailoring Software Engineering Generative AI Agents to Company Standards and Developers\u2019 Preferences"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-5713-4075","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pedro","family":"Fiuza Linhares","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"SDU Software Engineering, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, University of Southern Denmark, Odense, Denmark"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3774748.3787673","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T21:41:26Z","timestamp":1788990086000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3774748.3787673","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843282.3844430","title":["Schema-Conditioned Next-Tool Prediction for Coding Agents under Interface Drift"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-3136-1295","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Selma","family":"Nyberg","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Stockholm University, Stockholm, Sweden"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3056-6801","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Isak","family":"Samsten","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Stockholm University, Stockholm, Sweden"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843282.3844430","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:26:31Z","timestamp":1791451591000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843282.3844430","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3844569","title":["Beyond Outcomes: Toward Process-Aware Software Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-7264-268X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shuyang","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign, Champaign, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3844569","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3844569","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843282.3844445","title":["Documentation Retrieval by AI Coding Agents: An Empirical Study of HTTP Behavioral Signatures"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-2965-7661","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Oleksii","family":"Borysenko","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Cisco DevNet, Madrid, Spain"},{"name":"Cisco, Kyiv, Ukraine"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843282.3844445","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:26:31Z","timestamp":1791451591000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843282.3844445","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834536","title":["Command Coding: A Methodological Framework for AI-Accelerated Software Development"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-5150-5732","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Thomas S.","family":"Villani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"New Jersey Innovation Institute, Newark, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3185-2340","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Martin","family":"Kellogg","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"New Jersey Institute of Technology, Newark, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834536","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834536","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7508618","title":["AI-Assisted Software Engineering: Evaluating Copilot, Cursor, and Autonomous AI Agents for Rapid Application Development"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-9061-3470","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Muhammed","family":"Abiodun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of large language models into software development tooling has produced a spectrum of assistance paradigms, from inline code completion to autonomous multi-step agents capable of navigating repositories and executing commands. This paper presents an empirical evaluation of three representative systems GitHub Copilot, Cursor, and autonomous agentic IDEs across a rapid application development task set. We assess effectiveness (does the generated application satisfy stated requirements?), efficiency (time to completion relative to unaided baselines), iteration behavior (error detection and corrective actions), and code quality outcomes (vulnerability rates, technical debt indicators). Our synthesis of controlled trials, enterprise telemetry studies, and standardized benchmarks finds a consistent pattern: AI assistance substantially accelerates low-complexity, high-boilerplate development tasks, but performance degrades sharply as task horizon and cross-file coherence requirements increase. Autonomous agents complete only a small fraction of end-to-end feature development tasks that short-horizon benchmarks suggest they can handle. Security vulnerability rates in generated code remain high across tools. We conclude with a practical framework for selecting assistance paradigms by task characteristics, and with an assessment of the gap between marketed capability and demonstrated capability.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7508618","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]],"date-time":"2026-09-25T06:24:53Z","timestamp":1790317493000}},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Autonomous software engineering (SWE) agents powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved notable milestones on repository-scale benchmarks such as SWE-bench. However, prevailing evaluation methodologies rely almost exclusively on single-trial resolution rates (pass@1). In real-world software maintenance and continuous integration (CI\/CD) pipelines, single-trial success is an inadequate proxy for software reliability; an agent that succeeds on one invocation but fails on subsequent identical runs introduces non-deterministic flakiness and high human verification overhead. This paper contributes: (1) a prospective evaluation protocol designed to measure multi-trial consistency and trajectory pathologies across a 7,200-episode factorial grid (120 tasks \u00d7 6 models \u00d7 2 scaffolds \u00d7 5 trials), (2) formal metric formulations for empirical per-attempt pass rates ($\\hat{p}_{attempt}$), strict all-trial consistency (AllPass@k), completion rates ($CR, JCR_k$), and Patch Churn Ratio (PCR), and (3) an operational feasibility pilot across 312 completed episodes on Google Gemini Flash models using SWE-agent (ACI) and OpenHands (CodeAct). The pilot demonstrates the operational feasibility of trajectory-level telemetry collection and reveals a 13.3% infrastructure censoring rate (8.9% provider API timeouts, 4.4% container resets), establishing the measurement foundations required for subsequent full-scale empirical reliability benchmarking.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11115321\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]],"date-time":"2026-09-22T15:41:19Z","timestamp":1790091679000},"title":["An Empirical Investigation of Multi-Trial Consistency, Trajectory Pathologies, and Reliability Rankings in Software Engineering Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Tayyab","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Engineering and Technology Lahore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11115321\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11115321\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11115321\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]]}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7644118","title":["Causally Sound Memory Invalidation For Long-Horizon Software Engineering Agents: Transitive Constraints, Partial Observability, And Verification Budgets"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1401-7555","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Abdul","family":"Wasea","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Long-horizon coding agents depend on compressed or persistent memories of past tool outputs, repository observations, design constraints, and derived engineering decisions. When a repository changes, a remembered observation may remain historically true while no longer justifying an active decision. Existing retrieval, compression, and state-consistent memory systems address portions of this challenge, but reliable selective transitive invalidation under missing dependencies and bounded verification resources remains a sharply defined research target. We formulate engineering memory as a time-indexed, provenance-annotated justification hypergraph; distinguish observation validity, justification validity, and current action admissibility; and propose CAVI (Causal Assumption Verification and Invalidation), a research architecture combining change-impact analysis, dependency closure, uncertainty-aware verification, and guarded handoff. We specify conditional soundness guarantees for complete dependency graphs, illustrate unavoidable limits when dependencies are latent, and outline a reproducible benchmark with counterfactual repository mutations. The contribution here is a formal and experimental research agenda, not empirical evidence that CAVI outperforms prior systems.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7644118","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,21]],"date-time":"2026-10-21T15:09:57Z","timestamp":1792595397000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Penelitian ini membahas upaya prediksi konsumsi bahan bakar kendaraan berbasis algoritma machine learning untuk mendukung efisiensi energi dan pengurangan emisi karbon. Sektor transportasi merupakan kontributor signifikan terhadap konsumsi energi nasional, sehingga diperlukan model prediksi akurat yang dapat digunakan dalam perencanaan kebijakan. Penelitian ini bertujuan membandingkan kinerja tiga algoritma regresi Decision Tree (DT), Random Forest (RF), dan Support Vector Machine (SVM) dalam memprediksi konsumsi bahan bakar berdasarkan fitur teknis kendaraan. Dataset yang digunakan bersumber dari Natural Resources Canada, terdiri dari variabel seperti kapasitas mesin, silinder, transmisi, emisi CO\u2082, dan konsumsi bahan bakar. Proses pra-pemrosesan mencakup one-hot encoding, normalisasi, dan pembagian data (hold-out 80:20). Evaluasi dilakukan menggunakan metrik RMSE, MAE, dan koefisien determinasi (R\u00b2). Hasil menunjukkan bahwa model Decision Tree memiliki performa terbaik dengan nilai RMSE terendah (0.326), MAE terendah (0.22), dan R\u00b2 tertinggi (0.960), mengungguli Random Forest dan SVR. Temuan ini menunjukkan bahwa model sederhana namun tepat guna dapat lebih unggul dari model kompleks dalam kondisi dataset tertentu. Penelitian ini memberikan kontribusi terhadap pengembangan sistem prediktif efisiensi energi kendaraan dan dapat digunakan sebagai dasar untuk kebijakan transportasi berkelanjutan<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4379","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:57Z","timestamp":1792035177000},"title":["Perbandingan Kinerja Model Machine Learning dalam Memprediksi Konsumsi Bahan Bakar Kendaraan"],"author":[{"given":"Affan","family":"Prasetya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Oki","family":"Arifin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4379\/2690","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4379\/2690","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4379","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,31]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843282.3844426","title":["BDD-Bench: A Behavior-Driven Benchmark for Coding Agents across the Application Lifecycle"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7392-5923","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Micha\u0142","family":"Jureczka","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Jagiellonian University, Krakow, Poland"},{"name":"Exadel, Krakow, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1467-3017","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Anna","family":"Paleczek","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"AGH University of Krakow, Krakow, Poland"},{"name":"Exadel, Krakow, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-2689-3374","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Aiden","family":"Campbell","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Exadel, Warsaw, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8572-1469","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Karol","family":"Przystalski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Jagiellonian University, Krakow, Poland"},{"name":"Exadel, Krakow, Poland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843282.3844426","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:26:31Z","timestamp":1791451591000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843282.3844426","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>The transition from human-centric assistance to Autonomous Software Engineering (ASE) agents has enabled the resolution of complex real-world SE tasks. However, the trial-and-error nature of these agents generates lengthy interaction trajectories, creating severe bottlenecks in terms of context window limits and cost. While context compression offers a potential remedy, prior approaches suffer from static pruning strategies and granularity mismatches, often failing to preserve the semantic dependencies and syntactic details crucial for SE tasks. To strictly preserve critical task evidence while reducing context length, we introduce AttnCompress, a dynamic attention-guided trajectory compression framework. Unlike existing approaches, AttnCompress bridges the gap between semantic integrity and dynamic adaptability through three key mechanisms: (1) structure-aware segmentation via perplexity (PPL) spikes to preserve the syntactic structure of code and logs; (2) relevance estimation using proxy attention weights to quantify the precise relevance of historical blocks to the agent\u2019s current reasoning; and (3) a dynamic rolling window to re-evaluate and recall historical context as the task evolves. Extensive evaluation on SWE-Bench-Verified and Multi-SWE-Bench demonstrates that AttnCompress achieves a pass rate of 53.17%, outperforming prior state-of-the-art baselines while reducing token consumption by 21.6% and total costs by 33.6%. The framework proves to be model-agnostic and generalizes effectively across diverse programming languages.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3832149","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T19:32:02Z","timestamp":1790883122000},"title":["AttnCompress: Dynamic Attention-Guided Trajectory Compression for Software Engineering Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-8422-4522","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhengran","family":"Zeng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-0492-2234","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yixin","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1756-7746","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rui","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9331-4716","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wei","family":"Ye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8576-2674","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shikun","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832149","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832149","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3774748.3795666","title":["Test-Oriented Programming: rethinking coding for the GenAI era"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1303-4173","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jorge","family":"Melegati","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"INESC TEC, Faculty of Engineering, University of Porto, Porto, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3774748.3795666","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T21:41:26Z","timestamp":1788990086000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3774748.3795666","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Coding agents powered by large language models are becoming central modules of modern IDEs. They help users to perform various complex coding tasks by invoking tools. Although powerful, tool-invocation operation in coding agents opens a substantial attack surface for adversaries. Prior work has demonstrated attacks against both general-purpose LLM agents and domain-specific agents. However, to our knowledge, no previous works focus on the security risks of tool-invocation in coding agents. To fill this gap, we conduct the first systematic, in-depth red-teaming from a tool-invocation perspective in six popular real-world coding agents: Cursor, Claude Code, Copilot, Windsurf, Cline, and Trae.<\/jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>Our red-teaming proceeds in two phases. In Phase 1, we conduct a prompt leakage as reconnaissance to recover system prompts and related context. Specifically, we identify a mode gap between chat generation and tool-call argument generation: during schema-driven argument completion, the model behaves as if it is performing benign structured filling and may copy hidden agent context into tool-call arguments. We instantiate this gap as ToolLeak, which exfiltrates agent-internal prompts (e.g., system prompts and tool metadata) via required tool parameters. In Phase 2, we hijack the tool-invocation behavior of the coding agent with a novel two-channel prompt injection in the tool description and the tool return. Our hijacking achieves remote code execution (RCE) on major real-world coding agents. We adaptively construct the malicious payload using leaked security information in Phase 1.<\/jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>Our evaluation shows that ToolLeak substantially outperforms strong prompt-leak baselines in both emulated and real-world settings. In the emulated setting, ToolLeak achieves the best overall prompt-exfiltration performance across all six simulated coding agents. On real-world coding agents, ToolLeak achieves the best pseudo-recall on 18 of 25 evaluated agent-LLM pairs. Furthermore, our red-teaming successfully hijacks all six real-world coding agents for RCE and consistently yields higher attack success rates than baseline attacks. Lastly, we present two case studies on Cursor and Claude Code to demonstrate the real-world impact of our red-teaming.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3832267","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T19:32:02Z","timestamp":1790883122000},"title":["Red-Teaming Coding Agents from a Tool-Invocation Perspective: An Empirical Security Assessment"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-0436-8183","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuchong","family":"Xie","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-4965-0852","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mingyu","family":"Luo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fudan University, College of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence and Institute for Big Data, Shanghai, China"},{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-0776-8839","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zesen","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-4904-6333","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhixiang","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"},{"name":"Fudan University, College of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9784-6477","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kaikai","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2207-1569","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yu","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fudan University, College of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence and Institute for Big Data, Shanghai, China"},{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-4829-531X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ci","family":"Tao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fudan University, College of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence and Institute for Big Data, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-8821-6804","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Changhui","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fudan University, College of Computer Science and Artificial Intelligence and Institute for Big Data, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9897-4086","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zongjie","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8517-0580","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ping","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fudan University, Institute of Big Data, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0866-0308","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shuai","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-4549-332X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dongdong","family":"She","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Hong Kong, Hong Kong"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832267","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832267","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3774748.3795876","title":["Toward architecting self-coding information systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1222-0046","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rodrigo","family":"Falc\u00e3o","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Fraunhofer IESE, Kaiserslautern, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8748-3927","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Frank","family":"Elberzhager","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fraunhofer IESE, Kaiserslautern, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2317-6175","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Karthik","family":"Vaidhyanathan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"IIIT Hyderabad, Hyderabad, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3774748.3795876","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T21:41:26Z","timestamp":1788990086000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3774748.3795876","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843282.3844447","title":["They Do Not Fire Late, They Barely Fire"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-4827-9884","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Simarjot","family":"Khanna","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher, Montreal, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843282.3844447","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:26:31Z","timestamp":1791451591000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843282.3844447","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843282.3844425","title":["Context Inheritance: A Git-Native Architecture and Pre-registered Study of Repository Context for AI Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-4679-1036","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pavel","family":"Kerbel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Metatron Research, Tel Aviv, Israel"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-7893-9824","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Milana","family":"Kerbel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Metatron Research, Tel Aviv, Israel"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-0814-3822","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vitali","family":"Abramov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Metatron Research, Tel Aviv, Israel"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-1601-146X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Liat","family":"Abramov","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Metatron Research, Tel Aviv, Israel"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843282.3844425","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:26:31Z","timestamp":1791451591000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843282.3844425","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3793653.3793801","title":["Toward Architecture-Aware Evaluation Metrics for LLM Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-8534-028X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"D\u00e9bora","family":"Souza","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"SPLab, Federal University of Campina Grande, Campina Grande, Para\u00edba, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0661-0067","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Patr\u00edcia","family":"Machado","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"SPLab, Federal University of Campina Grande, Campina Grande, Para\u00edba, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3793653.3793801","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T16:41:57Z","timestamp":1789576917000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3793653.3793801","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Coding agents are given embedding search and code-graph tools on the premise that a repository's structure is something the model lacks and the tool supplies. We test that premise on SWE-bench Verified with small open-weight models, stratifying tasks by a deterministic hop depth (the shortest code-graph path from code the issue mentions to code the gold patch changes) that a companion paper defines and validates. First, we ablate a localization agent's tool set (grep only; +embedding search; +graph tools; all) on a depth-balanced 160-task split and on all 500 tasks for ten models (six Qwen3 sizes from 0.6B to 32B with five decodings each, an FP8 control, three Gemma 4 sizes; 79k episodes). Structured tools double grep-only accuracy for Qwen3-8B and Gemma 4 E4B; their gain peaks at 17-21 points at 1.7-4B and falls to 4-5 points at 32B (odds ratio 0.77-0.82 per doubling of parameters). From 8B up, about half of that gain is the form of the submission rather than finding the target; the fall from 14B to 32B is a fall in tool uptake at equal grep-only accuracy; and the same 4B size gains or does not gain from a tool depending on the checkpoint. Each extra hop multiplies the odds of correct localization by 0.50-0.67 at every size, and no tool set flattens that gradient: traces show the graph tools' gain is name lookup at hop 0 and one expansion at hop 1, and agents chain expansions in only 12-14% of episodes. A fixed repair model resolves 6-13 of 160 tasks from these localizations (oracle 26). Second, linear probes find direct call edges decodable under a within-anchor, degree-stratified protocol (AUC 0.74; 0.61-0.66 inside agent conversations), but below a lexical baseline (0.99), with two-hop edges at 0.57. Small agents carry a one-hop, largely lexical picture of their repository, in weights and in use. We release the agent, probes, logs and traces.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.66977\/xsci.2610.0030","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,27]],"date-time":"2026-10-27T22:27:32Z","timestamp":1793140052000},"title":["How Do Small Coding Agents Use Code Graphs and Embeddings?"],"author":[{"given":"Saurav","family":"Bellam","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/xsci.com\/preprint\/doi\/10.66977\/xsci.2610.0030.pdf","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.66977\/xsci.2610.0030","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,27]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3844573","title":["WiseSpec: Requirements-Driven Agents for Code Generation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9316-7250","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhao","family":"Tian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tianjin University, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3844573","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3844573","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3774748.3776593","title":["Designing Systems with Digital Twins and AI Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8538-7261","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bedir","family":"Tekinerdogan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Wageningen University, Wageningen, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3774748.3776593","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T21:41:26Z","timestamp":1788990086000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3774748.3776593","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202609.1250.v1","title":["SEBGen: A Self-Evolving Benchmark Generation Framework for Software Engineering Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-3232-4239","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jingjing","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4471-1977","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhaowen","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-8739-0514","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Daode","family":"Xiong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-3437-7604","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Changwei","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1618-6475","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yunxiao","family":"Geng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Static software engineering benchmarks such as SWE-bench suffer from three fundamental limitations: task sets are fixed and become saturated as model capabilities rapidly improve; expert construction is costly and slow; and difficulty levels cannot adapt to evolving model abilities. We present SEBGen, a self-evolving benchmark generation framework that synthesizes evaluation tasks from code repositories and improves its generation strategies through an evolution loop. SEBGen introduces three key innovations: an explicit five-dimension exploration value evaluator that quantitatively scores generation strategies across quality, novelty, difficulty coverage, skill diversity, and evolution potential, replacing implicit LLM judgments; a five-layer hybrid validation pipeline combining mechanical checks with LLM-as-judge evaluation; and a self-evolving loop with archive-based experience accumulation, sigmoid-scaled parent selection, and LLM-driven strategy mutation. On a synthetic corpus, SEBGen achieves a 96 percent validation pass rate with stable quality across five evolution generations. On a real repository, it transforms all six tqdm bug-fix commits into validated tasks, and a comparison against SWE-bench Lite shows task quality of 0.76 approaching expert baselines of 0.80. These results indicate that dynamic, self-evolving benchmarks are a viable alternative to static benchmarks.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202609.1250.v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,15]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T07:52:08Z","timestamp":1789545128000},"relation":{"is-preprint-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.3390\/electronics15204663","asserted-by":"subject"}]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843779.3844639","title":["Ranking Is Not Authority: Executable Context-Routing Contracts for Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-0864-6444","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nikhil","family":"Mahesh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent, Seattle, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843779.3844639","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:33:46Z","timestamp":1791452026000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843779.3844639","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834492","title":["Debugging the Debuggers: Failure-Anchored Structured Recovery for Software Engineering Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-1699-4206","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chenyu","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nankai University, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0330-0028","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shenglin","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nankai University, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-6490-7195","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yihang","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nankai University, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1096-2732","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wenwei","family":"Gu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nankai University, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-5941-5686","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhimin","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nankai University, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0266-7899","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yongqian","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nankai University, Tianjin, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5113-838X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dan","family":"Pei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tsinghua University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0102-8139","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chetan","family":"Bansal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Microsoft, Redmond, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2019-213X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Saravan","family":"Rajmohan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Microsoft, Redmond, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6303-1731","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Minghua","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Microsoft, Redmond, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834492","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834492","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3744916.3773140","title":["Beyond Final Code: A Process-Oriented Error Analysis of Software Development Agents in Real-World GitHub Scenarios"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-4631-9174","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhi","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0044-466X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wei","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4336-8548","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lingxiao","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3744916.3773140","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T12:11:14Z","timestamp":1789128674000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3744916.3773140","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Perjalanan kereta api yang panjang sering kali menimbulkan kebosanan bagi penumpang, terutama dengan minimnya fitur hiburan mandiri dan keterbatasan akses internet. Penelitian ini mengusulkan pengembangan aplikasi \"Railfun: Aplikasi Multimedia On Demand Berbasis Chatbot Sebagai Layanan Hiburan Pada Kereta Api\" untuk mengatasi permasalahan tersebut. Tujuan utama penelitian ini adalah mengembangkan aplikasi Railfun sebagai platform layanan Multimedia on Demand yang optimal bagi penumpang kereta api, serta mengintegrasikan fitur Chatbot untuk meningkatkan interaksi dan kemudahan pengguna. Aplikasi ini dirancang untuk menyediakan akses offline ke berbagai konten hiburan seperti video, musik, dan permainan, dilengkapi dengan Chatbot sebagai asisten virtual yang intuitif. Hasil evaluasi kepuasan pengguna menunjukkan bahwa 89,68% responden menyatakan puas dengan aplikasi Railfun, yang mengindikasikan kategori 'Sangat Memuaskan'. Diharapkan, Railfun dapat meningkatkan kenyamanan dan kepuasan penumpang selama perjalanan, sekaligus menjadi solusi inovatif dalam meningkatkan kualitas layanan transportasi publik melalui pemanfaatan teknologi digital yang modern dan efisien<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4424","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:57Z","timestamp":1792035177000},"title":["RAILFUN: Aplikasi Multimedia On Demand Berbasis Chat Bot Sebagai Layanan Hiburan Pada Kereta API"],"author":[{"given":"Riza","family":"Sukmawardani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sirli","family":"Fahriah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Afandi","family":"Nur Aziz Thohari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4424\/2688","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4424\/2688","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4424","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,5]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834337","title":["LLM-Based Agents for Identifying Bug-Introducing Commits"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0666-5025","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Niklas","family":"Risse","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"MPI-SP, Bochum, Germany"},{"name":"CISPA, Saarbr\u00fccken, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4470-1824","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marcel","family":"B\u00f6hme","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"MPI-SP, Bochum, Germany"},{"name":"CISPA, Saarbr\u00fccken, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834337","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834337","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.5040\/bci-0kg8.ch-012","title":["The Future of LLMs in Software Development"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5040\/bci-0kg8.ch-012","published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,8]],"date-time":"2026-09-08T12:09:36Z","timestamp":1788869376000}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Static software engineering benchmarks such as SWE-bench suffer from three fundamental limitations: task sets are fixed and become saturated as model capabilities rapidly improve; expert construction is costly and slow; and difficulty levels cannot adapt to evolving model abilities. We present SEBGen, a self-evolving benchmark generation framework that synthesizes evaluation tasks from code repositories and continuously updates its generation strategies through an evolution loop. SEBGen introduces three key innovations: an explicit five-dimension exploration value evaluator that quantitatively scores generation strategies across quality, novelty, difficulty coverage, skill diversity, and evolution potential, replacing implicit LLM judgments; a hybrid validation pipeline combining three mechanical checks (schema, compile, functional), an LLM-based semantic assessment, and a repeated-judgment gate; and a self-evolving loop with archive-based experience accumulation, sigmoid-scaled parent selection, and LLM-driven strategy mutation. On a synthetic corpus, SEBGen achieves a 96 percent stage-level validation pass rate (E1; 48\/50 stage observations) and preserves generation quality through the evolution loop while showing no measurable improvement over a budget-matched non-evolution baseline. On a real repository, it generates candidate benchmark tasks from all six tqdm commits that passed the schema and LLM-based screening stages, although the compile and functional stages could not be executed because these tasks carry no test patch. A judge-level comparison against SWE-bench Lite, partially corroborated by an independent judge from a different model family (Qwen), places generated real repository tasks below expert-constructed tasks (0.48 vs. 0.83 under deepseek-chat; 0.55 vs. 0.78 under qwen-plus, mean of two passes). An extension to two further repositories (requests, flask) achieves 0\/4 acceptance across executed screening stages, indicating that the system does not yet transfer reliably beyond the original repository. These results provide proof-of-concept evidence of feasibility: the framework generates candidate tasks automatically, while executable verification of real-repository tasks, reliable self-improvement, and cross-repository transfer remain open problems.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3390\/electronics15204663","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T11:53:02Z","timestamp":1791805982000},"title":["SEBGen: A Self-Evolving Benchmark Generation Framework for Software Engineering Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-3232-4239","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jingjing","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Aviation Industry Information Center, Beijing 100029, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"methodology","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"software","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"validation","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-original-draft","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4471-1977","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhaowen","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Aviation Industry Information Center, Beijing 100029, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"project-administration","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"given":"Daode","family":"Xiong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Aviation Industry Information Center, Beijing 100029, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"},{"role":"supervision","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"given":"Changwei","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Aviation Industry Information Center, Beijing 100029, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"}]},{"given":"Yunxiao","family":"Geng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Aviation Industry Information Center, Beijing 100029, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"},{"role":"writing-review-editing","vocabulary":"credit"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.mdpi.com\/2079-9292\/15\/20\/4663\/pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/electronics15204663","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Augmented Reality (AR) merupakan teknologi yang mampu mengintegrasikan objek virtual ke dalam lingkungan nyata secara real-time sehingga dapat memberikan pengalaman visual yang lebih interaktif. Selain visualisasi objek tiga dimensi, interaksi pengguna terhadap objek virtual menjadi salah satu aspek penting dalam pengembangan aplikasi AR. Penelitian ini bertujuan untuk mengimplementasikan sistem interaksi multi touch untuk manipulasi objek 3D pada aplikasi Augmented Reality berbasis Android. Metode pengembangan yang digunakan adalah Multimedia Development Life Cycle (MDLC) yang terdiri dari tahapan Concept, Design, Material Collecting, Assembly, Testing, dan Distribution, dengan fokus penelitian pada tahap Assembly dan Testing. Implementasi dilakukan menggunakan Unity dan Vuforia, di mana objek 3D ditempatkan pada Image target dan dilengkapi fitur manipulasi berupa rotasi menggunakan satu sentuhan (single touch) serta perubahan skala menggunakan dua sentuhan (pinch gesture). Pengujian dilakukan menggunakan metode Black Box Testing pada perangkat Redmi 13 dengan total 50 skenario pengujian. Hasil pengujian menunjukkan bahwa seluruh skenario berhasil dijalankan sesuai dengan fungsi yang dirancang sehingga memperoleh tingkat keberhasilan sebesar 100%. Berdasarkan hasil tersebut, sistem interaksi multi touch yang diimplementasikan mampu mendukung manipulasi objek 3D secara responsif dan intuitif pada lingkungan Augmented Reality berbasis Android serta berpotensi dikembangkan untuk berbagai kebutuhan aplikasi interaktif di masa mendatang.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5021","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:52Z","timestamp":1792035172000},"title":["Implementasi Sistem Interaksi Multi Touch untuk Manipulasi Objek 3D Berbasis Augmented Reality","Implementasi Sistem Interaksi Multi-touch untuk Manipulasi Objek 3D Berbasis Augmented Reality"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1977-3923","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yoseph","family":"Satria Praka","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ananda","family":"Maria","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Candi","family":"Rahayu Tri Prameswari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nurul","family":"Wahyuni","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Indah","family":"Sari Mukarramah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5021\/2794","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5021\/2794","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5021","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>: Sektor konstruksi informal pada proyek perumahan KPR sering kali menghadapi ketidaksesuaian informasi dan ketidakpastian arus kas yang membebani mandor melalui sistem dana talangan upah pekerja. Penelitian ini bertujuan untuk merancang, mengimplementasikan, dan menguji keandalan fungsional aplikasi mobile berbasis No-Code Development Platform (NCDP) AppSheet guna mendigitalisasi manajemen administrasi proyek secara inklusif. Metode penelitian yang diterapkan mengadopsi siklus pengembangan perangkat lunak terstruktur dengan pengujian fungsionalitas sistem menggunakan metode Black-Box Testing. Hasil penelitian menunjukkan bahwa arsitektur struktur tabel yang dibangun berhasil mentransformasi pencatatan absensi harian, manajemen kasbon, dan rekapitulasi upah buruh yang semula berbasis kertas menjadi sistem repositori cloud yang terpusat dan dinamis. Evaluasi pengujian membuktikan seluruh fungsi navigasi, validasi input data transaksional, serta otomatisasi formula perhitungan upah bersih berjalan presisi dan bebas dari kesalahan perhitungan, sehingga digitalisasi proses bisnis melalui platform no-code membuktikan bahwa teknologi nir-kode layak menjadi solusi digital terjangkau yang mudah diadopsi oleh para pelaku industri di sektor konstruksi informal.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5108","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:52Z","timestamp":1792035172000},"title":["Implementasi No Code Platform pada Aplikasi Pencatatan Absen dan Gaji Pekerja dalam Proyek Pembangunan Perumahan KPR"],"author":[{"given":"Rasmiati","family":"Rasyid","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rabiah","family":"Adawiyah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mardiawati","family":"Mardiawati","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5108\/2841","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5108\/2841","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5108","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3793653.3793776","title":["How to Build AI Agents by Augmenting LLMs with Codified Human Expert Domain Knowledge? A Software Engineering Framework"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-3748-3273","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Choro","family":"Ulan uulu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, Eindhoven University of Technology, M\u00fcnchen, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-0440-030X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mikhail","family":"Kulyabin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, M\u00fcnchen, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-0007-4575","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Iris","family":"Fuhrmann","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, M\u00fcnchen, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-2503-4741","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jan","family":"Joosten","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, M\u00fcnchen, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2198-7823","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nuno Miguel Martins","family":"Pacheco","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, M\u00fcnchen, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-4191-6962","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Filippos","family":"Petridis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, M\u00fcnchen, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-8652-9003","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rebecca","family":"Johnson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, M\u00fcnchen, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2854-722X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jan","family":"Bosch","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chalmers University of Technology, Eindhoven University of Technology, G\u00f6teborg, Sweden"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7700-1816","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Helena Holmstr\u00f6m","family":"Olsson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Malm\u00f6 University, Malmo, Sweden"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3793653.3793776","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T16:41:57Z","timestamp":1789576917000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3793653.3793776","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.26855\/ea.2026.09.016","title":["Research on Automatic Analysis of Software Testing Results and Defect Feedback Mechanisms Based on Intelligent Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Yue","family":"Cheng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26855\/ea.2026.09.016","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,17]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,22]],"date-time":"2026-10-22T08:49:23Z","timestamp":1792658963000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3793653.3793793","title":["Managing Variability in Industrial AI Agents for Manufacturing: Experiences at Hitachi"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-0827-3441","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kentaro","family":"Yoshimura","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Hitachi, Ltd., Ibaraki, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-6725-3197","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Eiji","family":"Nomura","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hitachi, Ltd., Tokyo, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-7911-0782","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hiroshi","family":"Yoshikawa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hitachi, Ltd., Tokyo, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3793653.3793793","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T16:41:57Z","timestamp":1789576917000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3793653.3793793","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7609705","title":["Gated Autonomy: A Layered Security Framework for Tool Access Governance in LLM-Driven Coding Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-1027-2046","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rut","family":"Patel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Model (LLM)-driven coding agents have evolved from inline code completion utilities into autonomous systems capable of executing shell commands, modifying file hierarchies, retrieving remote documentation, and invoking external APIs. This expanded operational autonomy introduces security vulnerabilities that conventional software permission architectures fail to mediate. In this paper, we examine the attack surface created when autonomous programming agents are granted broad access to system-level tools. We propose Gated Autonomy, an architectural framework that enforces the principle of least privilege across the entire tool-execution lifecycle. The framework couples three defensive layers: a declarative policy gate enforcing static capability boundaries and parameter whitelisting, a runtime context evaluator tracking data provenance and privilege accumulation, and a postexecution audit engine providing counterfactual verification against temporal causal manipulation. We evaluate Gated Autonomy against a comprehensive taxonomy of fifteen documented attack vectors drawn from commercial coding assistants and open-source agent runtimes. Our empirical analysis demonstrates that the framework completely mitigates direct tool hijacking and cross-tool privilege escalation while reducing developer verification interruptions by over 80% through selective escalation.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7609705","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,14]],"date-time":"2026-10-14T14:42:09Z","timestamp":1791988929000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tse.2026.3736694","title":["Detecting Non-Optimal Decisions of Embodied Agents via Diversity-Guided Metamorphic Testing"],"author":[{"given":"Wenzhao","family":"Wu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanyang Technological University","department":["College of Computing and Data Science"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yahui","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Chongqing University of Posts and Telecommunications","place":["Chongqing, P. R. China"],"department":["School of Computer"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mingfei","family":"Cheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University","department":["School of Computing and Information Systems"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenbing","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northwest A&F University","place":["Yangling, Shaanxi, P. R. China"],"department":["College of Information Engineering"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuan","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang Sci-Tech University","place":["Hangzhou, Zhejiang, P. R. China"],"department":["School of Computer Science and Technology"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanyang Technological University","department":["College of Computing and Data Science"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tse.2026.3736694","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,22]],"date-time":"2026-09-22T19:11:56Z","timestamp":1790104316000},"link":[{"URL":"http:\/\/xplorestaging.ieee.org\/ielx8\/32\/4359463\/11701704.pdf?arnumber=11701704","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834557","title":["Agents That Teach: Towards Designing Incidental Learning Back into AI-Assisted Software Development"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9362-950X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rohit","family":"Mehra","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture Labs, Bengaluru, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-2500-9816","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Samdyuti","family":"Suri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture Labs, Bengaluru, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1655-6153","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Prithviraj K.","family":"Tagadinamani","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture Labs, Bengaluru, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-5785-9509","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kapil","family":"Singi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture Labs, Bengaluru, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9774-6265","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vikrant","family":"Kaulgud","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture Labs, Bengaluru, India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-3627-588X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Adam P.","family":"Burden","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Accenture, St. Petersburg, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834557","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834557","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2172\/17425826","title":["Skills Before Smarts: Harness Engineering for Robust Software-Development Agents"],"author":[{"name":"Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory (LLNL), Livermore, CA (United States)","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hari","family":"Hariharan Devarajan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"name":"USDOE National Nuclear Security Administration (NNSA)","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2172\/17425826","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,24]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,24]],"date-time":"2026-10-24T05:06:53Z","timestamp":1792818413000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3844572","title":["Toward Authentic and Scalable Evaluation of Terminal Agents in Real-World Environments"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4333-8063","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhaoyang","family":"Chu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University College London, London, United Kingdom"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3844572","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3844572","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3837535","title":["Safeguarding LLM Agents from Misalignment through Provenance Analysis"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0071-501X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yining","family":"She","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-6562-7166","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yiliang","family":"Liang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7891-6885","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Eunsuk","family":"Kang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3837535","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3837535","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>The combination of LLMs and autonomous agent architectures has led to assertive but exaggerated claims about how they can revolutionise the automation of software engineering. This review critically reviews the journey of LLM-based software engineering agents from 2015 to 2025, combining insights from verified more than 20 primary studies to investigate true capabilities, benchmark outputs and documented failures.\n\u00a0Our comparative studies are original, being done in a total of six architectural classes from a simple pipeline system to a complex multi-agent collaborative framework. We provide a quantitative comparison of resolution rate, cost, and failure taxonomy. An important conclusion is the Agentless Paradox: structurally simpler approaches match and outperform autonomous agents on the dominant SWE-bench benchmark, with resolution rates of leading systems reaching 75% on SWE-bench Verified (2025) versus &lt;5% at the benchmark\u2019s 2023 launch. We categorize six modes of failures: hallucination, context overflow, test overfitting, over-engineering, inter-agent communication errors, and security boundary violations. We then critically assess how well the state-of-the-art architectural solutions address each of them.\u00a0 Further research is required in benchmarking ecological validity, adversarial robustness, and safety auditing. These gaps are priority issues.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.65204\/djes.v3i3.828","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,28]],"date-time":"2026-10-28T20:22:05Z","timestamp":1793218925000},"title":["Demystifying LLM-Based Software Engineering Agents: A Review of Capabilities, Benchmarks, and Failure Modes"],"author":[{"given":"Hasan","family":"Fadhil qasim","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/journal.duc.edu.iq\/index.php\/djst\/article\/download\/828\/620","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/journal.duc.edu.iq\/index.php\/djst\/article\/download\/828\/620","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.65204\/djes.v3i3.828","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,26]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Perkembangan pesat AI generatif seperti ChatGPT membawa dampak besar pada pendidikan tinggi, terutama dalam sistem pembelajaran terbuka dan jarak jauh (ODL). Penelitian menunjukkan bahwa meskipun ChatGPT menawarkan keuntungan seperti akses informasi cepat, bantuan penulisan, dan personalisasi pembelajaran, penggunaan yang tidak bijak dapat menurunkan motivasi belajar, memicu ketergantungan, serta menghambat berpikir kritis dan kreativitas mahasiswa. Selain itu, muncul tantangan etis seperti plagiarisme, pelanggaran privasi, dan kurangnya literasi AI. Oleh karena itu, diperlukan strategi pemanfaatan AI yang adaptif dan etis untuk mendukung pembelajaran tanpa mengorbankan kemampuan akademik dan intelektual mahasiswa.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4363","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:57Z","timestamp":1792035177000},"title":["Peran ChatGPT dalam Meningkatkan dan Menghambat Proses Belajar Mahasiswa di Era Digital"],"author":[{"given":"Dya","family":"Arzepta Maiza","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nayla","family":"Sifa Syabila","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Salwa","family":"Aprila","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Harun","family":"Mukhtar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rahmad","family":"Gunawan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Januar","family":"Al Amien","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4363\/2689","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4363\/2689","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4363","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,20]]}},{"DOI":"10.29327\/45981342","title":["O VIBE CODING COMO NOVO PARADIGMA DA ENGENHARIA DE SOFTWARE: UMA REVISAO BIBLIOGRAFICA"],"author":[{"given":"MICHEL DE","family":"SOUZA","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p xmlns:lang=\"pt-br\">Este trabalho examina o Vibe Coding como pr\u00e1tica emergente no campo da Engenharia de Software, considerando sua rela\u00e7\u00e3o com a intelig\u00eancia artificial generativa, os modelos avan\u00e7ados de linguagem e os ambientes de desenvolvimento apoiados por agentes. A pesquisa, de natureza bibliogr\u00e1fica, qualitativa, explorat\u00f3ria e descritiva, foi conduzida como revis\u00e3o narrativa da literatura, com procedimentos organizados de busca, sele\u00e7\u00e3o e classifica\u00e7\u00e3o das fontes. O problema central consiste em compreender de que modo essa forma de programa\u00e7\u00e3o assistida por linguagem natural modifica pr\u00e1ticas tradicionais de desenvolvimento e em que medida pode ser interpretada como poss\u00edvel mudan\u00e7a paradigm\u00e1tica. Para isso, s\u00e3o articulados fundamentos cl\u00e1ssicos da Engenharia de Software, estudos recentes sobre modelos de linguagem aplicados ao c\u00f3digo e pesquisas brasileiras relacionadas a requisitos, testes, ensino de programa\u00e7\u00e3o e governan\u00e7a da intelig\u00eancia artificial. A an\u00e1lise indica que o fen\u00f4meno n\u00e3o se restringe \u00e0 gera\u00e7\u00e3o autom\u00e1tica de c\u00f3digo, pois desloca parte da atua\u00e7\u00e3o profissional para a formula\u00e7\u00e3o de inten\u00e7\u00f5es, constru\u00e7\u00e3o de contexto, revis\u00e3o cr\u00edtica, valida\u00e7\u00e3o, seguran\u00e7a e governan\u00e7a dos artefatos produzidos. Ao mesmo tempo, a literatura aponta riscos relevantes, como baixa qualidade, vulnerabilidades, confian\u00e7a excessiva, depend\u00eancia tecnol\u00f3gica, problemas de privacidade, propriedade intelectual e d\u00e9bito t\u00e9cnico. Conclui-se que o Vibe Coding apresenta ind\u00edcios de transforma\u00e7\u00e3o nas pr\u00e1ticas de desenvolvimento, mas sua classifica\u00e7\u00e3o como novo paradigma ainda exige cautela, uma vez que o conceito permanece recente, pouco estabilizado academicamente e dependente dos fundamentos consolidados da Engenharia de Software.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.29327\/45981342","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,27]],"date-time":"2026-10-27T17:13:07Z","timestamp":1793121187000}},{"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                <jats:p>\n                  Data curation\u2014the process of collecting, cleaning, and joining raw datasets for unified analysis\u2014is a time-consuming yet crucial part of any data science project. With the emergence of powerful agentic coding tools, it is tempting to \u201cvibe curate\u2014\n                  <jats:italic>i<\/jats:italic>\n                  .\n                  <jats:italic>e<\/jats:italic>\n                  ., to prompt AI agents to perform data curation operations and blindly trust their outputs in order to finish work more quickly. However, this can exacerbate two key challenges that already plague manual data curation workflows:\n                  <jats:bold>reproducibility<\/jats:bold>\n                  \u2014the ability to trace the exact set of modifications applied to raw datasets\u2014and\n                  <jats:bold>verifiability<\/jats:bold>\n                  \u2014the ability to audit changes and confirm that data is processed properly. To address these issues, we introduce\n                  <jats:bold>Scrub Data<\/jats:bold>\n                  , a framework that enables the use of AI agents in data curation workflows while transparently maintaining both reproducibility and verifiability. The framework involves an interactive data curation loop between a user and an AI agent, backed by a data provenance graph that tracks and versions all dataset updates. The data graph requires that all updates are formatted as self-contained executable steps, allowing any version of a dataset to be reconstructed from raw data by playing forward the transformations stored in the graph. This workflow takes place within a lightweight web application that is designed to be modified by users and agents in real-time to create custom visualization tools for identifying data curation needs and verifying outcomes. We demonstrate the usefulness of the framework with a real data curation use case from animal movement ecology. Starting with 89M raw GPS coordinates, we use the framework to curate a benchmark dataset of 2.5M coordinates to be used for machine learning or statistical analysis. The framework is available for use and extension at\n                  <jats:ext-link xmlns:xlink=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1999\/xlink\" ext-link-type=\"uri\" xlink:href=\"https:\/\/github.com\/justinkay\/scrubdata\">https:\/\/github.com\/justinkay\/scrubdata<\/jats:ext-link>\n                  .\n                <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.64898\/2026.09.19.752880","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]],"date-time":"2026-09-23T17:20:26Z","timestamp":1790184026000},"title":["Scrub Data: A Framework for Reproducible Data Curation with AI Coding Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Justin","family":"Kay","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"MIT"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shir","family":"Bar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"MIT"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sara","family":"Beery","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"MIT"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/syndication.highwire.org\/content\/doi\/10.64898\/2026.09.19.752880","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.64898\/2026.09.19.752880","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,23]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.ascom.2026.101184","title":["Agents for astronomical software and astronomical software for agents: Bidirectional enhancement in development and scientific reliability"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8379-4736","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Haiwen","family":"Xie","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-6801-5716","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lei","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yewen","family":"Yin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-1233-3563","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jian","family":"Ge","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jianwu","family":"Rui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.ascom.2026.101184","published":{"date-parts":[[2027,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,26]],"date-time":"2026-09-26T15:30:29Z","timestamp":1790436629000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S2213133726001265?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S2213133726001265?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834438","title":["Free Is More: Empowering Software Agents via Issue-Free Trajectory Learning and Entropy-Aware RLVR Training"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2115-9921","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xin-Cheng","family":"Wen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Ant Group, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1155-3483","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Binbin","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Ant Group, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-3635-4417","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Haoxuan","family":"Lan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5818-686X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hang","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Ant Group, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5799-5876","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Peng","family":"Di","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Ant Group, Hangzhou, China"},{"name":"University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4774-2434","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Cuiyun","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834438","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834438","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834386","title":["Fragmented Markers, Mixed Results: A Systematic Review of AI Coding Assistants and Developer Productivity"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7051-2242","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Annemarie","family":"Wittig","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Leipzig University, Leipzig, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6204-2095","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Alina","family":"Mailach","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Leipzig University, Leipzig, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7741-7777","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Norbert","family":"Siegmund","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Leipzig University, Leipzig, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834386","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834386","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7397283","title":["Legacy System Modernization with Coding Agents: A Case Study"],"author":[{"given":"Iago","family":"da Silva Rodrigues Alves","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0206-1056","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Cristiano","family":"Politowski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3371-7353","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Jo\u00e3o  Eduardo","family":"Montandon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Legacy systems built on discontinued platforms are a recurring technological liability in organizations that depend on these applications to sustain critical business processes. Although modernization is strategically necessary, it is costly and error-prone when performed exclusively through manual effort. In this paper, we report on a case study conducted in a real industrial setting, where we evaluate both the effectiveness and efficiency of AI coding agents in supporting the migration of legacy systems to modern platforms. By using one version of Claude Code agent, we migrated 12 features of distinct complexity levels from a corporate ERP system written in Visual Basic 6 to C# .NET 10 under a single-generation strategy. Once the migration sessions were completed, we measured the equivalence of the migrated features against the original ones, and collected data about the time spent and the number of tokens consumed by the agent during the process. In our experiment, the agent achieved an average equivalence of 70%, with a strong asymmetry across complexity levels; low-level features achieved 92%, and high-complexity ones scored 47%. Similar asymmetry was observed in the cost-based metrics; low-level features consumed 1.47M tokens ($1.66), whereas high-level ones used 9.09M ($10.28). We reveal the practical scenarios and circumstances where this migration strategy is more effective, as well as discuss the limitations and challenges of using AI agents for legacy system modernization.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7397283","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]],"date-time":"2026-09-02T17:44:59Z","timestamp":1788371099000}},{"DOI":"10.3139\/9783446488540.fm","title":["Von Vibe Coding zu Agentic Engineering"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3139\/9783446488540.fm","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,4]],"date-time":"2026-09-04T13:46:49Z","timestamp":1788529609000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3843779.3844634","title":["Agent4RE: A Self-Refining Multi-agent Framework for End-to-End Software Requirements Engineering and Benchmarking"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-8670-6843","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yongjian","family":"Tang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, Research and Predevelopment, Munich, Germany"},{"name":"Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-9343-3992","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Linhan","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5465-198X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Thomas","family":"Runkler","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Siemens AG, Munich, Germany"},{"name":"Technical University of Munich, Munich, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3843779.3844634","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,8]],"date-time":"2026-10-08T09:33:46Z","timestamp":1791452026000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3843779.3844634","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834395","title":["ExplainBench: Evaluating Code Explanations from Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-6059-5191","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhiyuan","family":"Pan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0298-5320","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sungmin","family":"Kang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0900-5230","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Imam Nur Bani","family":"Yusuf","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7127-1137","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Abhik","family":"Roychoudhury","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National University of Singapore, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834395","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834395","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834632","title":["LintCFG: DSL-Driven Linter Configuration Generation for Coding Standards"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-5005-7485","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yi","family":"Tang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University, \u5357\u4eac\u5e02, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-4397-8562","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zejun","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University, \u5357\u4eac\u5e02, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7663-1421","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhenchang","family":"Xing","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"CSIRO's Data61, Canberra, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4011-5350","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Minxue","family":"Pan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University, \u5357\u4eac\u5e02, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0104-2731","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tian","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University, \u5357\u4eac\u5e02, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3090-9568","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xuandong","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Nanjing University, \u5357\u4eac\u5e02, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834632","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834632","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Email telah menjadi alat komunikasi vital, namun efektivitasnya terdegradasi oleh volume spam yang tinggi. Algoritma Naive Bayes (NB) adalah metode machine learning yang populer untuk klasifikasi spam. Namun, kinerja NB dapat dipengaruhi oleh tingginya dimensi data (jumlah fitur kata yang sangat banyak). Penelitian ini bertujuan untuk menganalisis dampak seleksi fitur Chi-Square \u00a0terhadap kinerja Naive Bayes. Metode penelitian ini adalah analisis komparatif antara dua model: (A) Naive Bayes standar, dan (B) Naive Bayes dengan seleksi fitur Chi-Square. Kedua model menggunakan vektorisasi TF-IDF pada dataset Enron yang berisi 33.716 email. Model A menggunakan 5.000 fitur, sedangkan Model B direduksi menjadi 1.000 fitur terbaik hasil seleksi . Hasil penelitian menunjukkan bahwa Model A (tanpa ) telah mencapai akurasi sangat tinggi sebesar 98,1%. Model B (dengan ) mampu mencapai akurasi yang hampir identik sebesar 98,0%, dengan nilai presisi dan recall yang juga serupa. Kesimpulan dari penelitian ini adalah penerapan Chi-Square pada kasus ini tidak meningkatkan akurasi (karena model dasar sudah mencapai kinerja puncak), namun terbukti sangat efektif dalam mereduksi dimensi fitur sebesar 80% (dari 5.000 menjadi 1.000 fitur) sambil tetap mempertahankan kinerja model. Hal ini menunjukkan bahwa kombinasi NB+\u00a0menghasilkan model yang jauh lebih efisien secara komputasi.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4572","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:57Z","timestamp":1792035177000},"title":["Analisis Komparatif Kinerja Algoritma Naive Bayes dengan dan tanpa Seleksi Fitur Chi-Square untuk Deteksi Spam Email"],"author":[{"given":"Fitra","family":"Salam S. Nagalay","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Desi","family":"Rahma Aryanti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fathurrahman","family":"Kurniawan Ikhsan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4572\/2681","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4572\/2681","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4572","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,15]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Persediaan barang merupakan salah satu aspek penting dalam operasional usaha karena berpengaruh terhadap kelancaran proses penjualan dan ketersediaan produk bagi konsumen. Toko Aulia masih mengalami kendala dalam pengelolaan stok barang yang dilakukan secara kurang terstruktur sehingga berpotensi menyebabkan kekurangan maupun kelebihan persediaan. Penelitian ini bertujuan merancang sistem informasi persediaan barang berbasis web dengan menerapkan metode Monte Carlo untuk memprediksi kebutuhan stok barang. Metode penelitian yang digunakan meliputi observasi, wawancara, studi literatur, serta pengembangan sistem menggunakan pendekatan SDLC (System Development Life Cycle). Sistem dirancang menggunakan pemodelan UML dan diimplementasikan berbasis web untuk mempermudah proses pengelolaan data. Metode Monte Carlo digunakan untuk melakukan simulasi dan prediksi kebutuhan stok berdasarkan data historis penjualan. Hasil penelitian menunjukkan bahwa sistem yang dirancang mampu membantu proses pengelolaan persediaan barang secara lebih terstruktur serta mendukung pengambilan keputusan dalam menentukan jumlah kebutuhan stok pada periode berikutnya. Dengan adanya sistem ini, diharapkan proses pengendalian persediaan menjadi lebih efektif dan efisien.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5220","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:52Z","timestamp":1792035172000},"title":["Perancangan Sistem Informasi Persediaan Barang Berbasis Web Menggunakan Metode Monte Carlo Untuk Prediksi Kebutuhan Stok Barang"],"author":[{"given":"Sovia","family":"Sovia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fanny","family":"Septiani Bufra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuli","family":"Hartati","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fauzan","family":"Azim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Oki","family":"Arifin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5220\/2843","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5220\/2843","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5220","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Pengelompokan tipe kepribadian menjadi salah satu cara penting untuk memahami bagaimana individu berinteraksi dalam konteks sosial. Penelitian ini mengeksplorasi penerapan algoritma pembelajaran mesin dalam membedakan individu ke dalam dua kategori kepribadian utama: introvert dan ekstrovert, berdasarkan indikator perilaku sosial yang dapat diukur secara kuantitatif. Dataset yang digunakan mencakup 2.900 observasi dengan variabel-variabel seperti durasi waktu yang diluangkan secara mandiri, frekuensi partisipasi dalam kegiatan sosial, tingkat interaksi di luar lingkungan rumah, serta jumlah teman dalam jaringan sosial. Sebelum analisis, data mengalami proses pra-pemrosesan yang meliputi konversi variabel kategorikal menjadi bentuk numerik dan penskalaan fitur numerik agar seragam. Model klasifikasi yang digunakan adalah Random Forest Classifier dengan parameter baku, yang divalidasi menggunakan pembagian data latih dan uji melalui teknik stratified split untuk menjaga keseimbangan distribusi kelas. Hasil evaluasi menunjukkan performa model yang baik, dengan tingkat akurasi sebesar 90,86% dan F1-Score mencapai 91,03%. Temuan ini menegaskan bahwa pendekatan berbasis pembelajaran mesin, terutama model ensemble seperti Random Forest memiliki potensi besar dalam mengidentifikasi ciri kepribadian melalui pola perilaku sosial yang terekam.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4368","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:57Z","timestamp":1792035177000},"title":["Penerapan Random Forest untuk Identifikasi Tipe Kepribadian: Studi Kasus Klasifikasi Introvert dan Extrovert"],"author":[{"given":"Fauzan","family":"Azim","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Doni","family":"Iskandar Rotama Sihaloho","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Rizki","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Afdanil","family":"Hidayat Alghifary","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Husnul","family":"Qadri Habibullah","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Harya","family":"Jaya Kusuma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rahmad","family":"Gunawan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4368\/2680","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4368\/2680","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v1i2.4368","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,10]]}},{"DOI":"10.22214\/ijraset.2026.84804","title":["AI Coding Agents on GitHub: Repository Popularity as a Hidden Confounder in the Acceptance of 2.4 Million Agent-Authored Pull Requests"],"author":[{"given":"Perseus","family":"Bhavnagri","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Autonomous coding agents now open pull requests on GitHub without a person writing the code, and the share of\nthose pull requests that maintainers merge has become the usual shorthand for how well the agents work. This paper argues that\nthe shorthand measures the projects as much as it measures the agents. Using the public AIDev dataset, a snapshot of 2,743,854\npull requests written by six agents across 326,798 repositories between December 2024 and October 2025, we find that the merge\nrate falls from 91.58 percent in repositories with no stars to 60.05 percent in repositories with a thousand or more. The decline\nappears inside every agent separately, and in the five agents with enough volume in the top band to measure it the fall is between\n20.3 and 27.6 percentage points, so it cannot be an artefact of which agent is used where. The enriched portion of the dataset\nthat previously published analyses rely on contains only repositories with at least 100 stars, which we verify directly; it holds 2.7\npercent of the decided pull requests and merges them at 73.67 percent against 90.63 percent elsewhere. Holding the repository\nfixed with a Mantel-Haenszel estimator changes the picture between agents: of fifteen comparable pairs, two reverse direction\nand one loses more than half its effect.\nTime to a decision separates the same way, a median of 51 seconds in unstarred repositories against 5.4 hours in the most\npopular ones, with a medium effect size. Even within the enriched subset, 75.8 percent of agent pull requests carry no recorded\nhuman review. We conclude that published acceptance figures describe a narrow and unusually demanding slice of GitHub, and\nwe recommend that studies of agent contributions report the popularity distribution of their repositories and estimate effects\nwithin repositories rather than across them.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22214\/ijraset.2026.84804","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T11:26:03Z","timestamp":1788780363000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3744916.3764546","title":["AgentSpec: Customizable Runtime Enforcement for Safe and Reliable LLM Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-6379-5312","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Haoyu","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9376-2471","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Christopher M.","family":"Poskitt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3545-1392","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jun","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University, Singapore, Singapore"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3744916.3764546","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T12:11:14Z","timestamp":1789128674000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3744916.3764546","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3844559","title":["PCLA-Bridge: The Reusability of CARLA-Trained Autonomous Driving Agents beyond the CARLA Simulator"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3422-4750","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Masoud","family":"Jamshidiyan Tehrani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Universit\u00e0 della Svizzera italiana, Lugano, Switzerland"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3844559","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3844559","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Purpose:\nSoftware projects are increasingly using repository-level governance artifacts, such as \\texttt{CLAUDE.md} and \\texttt{AGENTS.md}, to communicate project-specific guidance to AI coding agents. Although these artifacts have become an important mechanism for governing AI coding agents, little is known about the governance practices and liabilities they encode, or whether their observable characteristics are consistent with organizational governance capability.\nMethod:\nTo examine these questions, we conducted an exploratory sequential mixed-methods study of 555 AI agent governance artifacts collected from public GitHub repositories. Using Socio-Technical Grounded Theory for Data Analysis (STGT4DA), we developed taxonomies of governance practices and governance liabilities, which were converted into a structured codebook for LLM-based classification. The codebook was evaluated against manually coded reference labels and applied to the corpus to examine whether these artifacts exhibit observable characteristics theoretically associated with organizational governance capability.\nResults:\nThe findings reveal that these artifacts primarily emphasize knowledge transfer, process guidance, and coding standards, whereas explicit oversight mechanisms, such as human approval gates, remain comparatively limited. The analysis also identifies recurring governance liabilities, most commonly vague instructions (36%) and fragmentation debt (35%). We found no systematic association between the governance observed in the artifacts and the repository maturity indicators examined in this study, nor did governance vary systematically across the application domains examined as broad proxies for potential operational consequences. The artifacts also showed limited evidence of characteristics associated with institutionalized governance, particularly regarding accountability mechanisms, coherence between governance content and governance quality, and project-specific adaptation.\nConclusion:\nThis study provides an empirical investigation of repository-level AI agent governance from an organizational capability perspective. By examining governance artifacts according to the governance functions they perform rather than solely the information they contain, the study develops taxonomies of governance practices and liabilities and an evaluated LLM-based classification approach for systematically characterizing AI agent governance in research and practice.<\/p>","DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11201839\/v1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,27]],"date-time":"2026-10-27T08:04:38Z","timestamp":1793088278000},"title":["Governing AI Coding Agents: An Empirical Analysis of Repository Artifacts"],"author":[{"given":"Muhammad","family":"Hamza","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"LUT University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wardah Naeem","family":"Awan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"LUT University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaofeng","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"LUT University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dominik","family":"Siemon","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"LUT University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11201839\/v1","content-type":"text\/html","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/www.researchsquare.com\/article\/rs-11201839\/v1.html","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-11201839\/v1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,27]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834519","title":["REAP: Automatic Curation of Coding Agent Benchmarks from Interactive Production Usage"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-7097-2226","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Smriti","family":"Jha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Meta Platforms, New York City, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2266-453X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Matteo","family":"Paltenghi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Meta Platforms, Menlo Park, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9432-1045","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chandra","family":"Maddila","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Meta Platforms, Bellevue, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-1374-7334","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vijayaraghavan","family":"Murali","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Meta Platforms, Bellevue, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9088-887X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shubham","family":"Ugare","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Meta Platforms, New York City, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2546-9000","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Satish","family":"Chandra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Meta Platforms, Menlo Park, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834519","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834519","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834363","title":["LLM Agents Can See Code Repositories"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-7996-492X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dongjian","family":"Ma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-9914-4172","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Silin","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-1621-0497","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yufei","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Xi'an Jiaotong University, Xi'an, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-9738-7072","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuling","family":"Shi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-2475-6802","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yanfu","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Zhejiang University, The State Key Laboratory of Blockchain and Data Security, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0529-6408","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiaodong","family":"Gu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834363","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834363","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.24908\/iqurcp21820","title":["Disclosure Failure in Compromised AI Coding Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Aaron","family":"Su","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rongxing","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Software developers increasingly delegate routine programming work to autonomous AI coding agents that read untrusted project files, run commands, and call external services with limited human review. No developer can audit every action such an agent takes, so oversight defaults to a single artifact: the summary the agent writes when it finishes. The completeness of that summary is a security property in its own right, yet current evaluations do not measure it. Existing benchmarks ask whether a malicious instruction planted in a repository succeeds in redirecting an agent. They do not ask whether the agent then discloses what it did.\nThis study takes up the second question. We instrument an open-source coding agent running against a controlled, sandboxed repository carrying an injected instruction, and establish per-trial ground truth on what the agent actually did from channels independent of its own account. The design covers nine models, with a second agent used to check that the effect reproduces. Setting that ground truth against the report the developer receives separates two behaviours that prior work has treated as one: whether an attack succeeds, and whether the agent discloses it.\nIn our data, resisting attack more often does not make a model more forthcoming when an attack does succeed. The two vary independently, so a benchmark scored on attack success alone cannot rank agents on the risk a developer actually carries. We find preliminary evidence that a single sentence added to the developer's own prompt substantially improves disclosure.Faculty Supervisor: Rongxing Lu<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.24908\/iqurcp21820","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,1]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,2]],"date-time":"2026-09-02T04:03:01Z","timestamp":1788321781000}},{"DOI":"10.70593\/978-81-6632-094-1","title":["Code You Didn't Write, Risk You Still Own: Governing LLM Coding Assistants in Regulated Financial Software"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-8578-9321","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ashutosh","family":"Pal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70593\/978-81-6632-094-1","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,30]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,2]],"date-time":"2026-10-02T12:09:30Z","timestamp":1790942970000}},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7601279","title":["Decommissioning Enterprise Software: AI Agents and the Collapse of the Replication Moat"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-5872-7840","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Elliot","family":"Saha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;I have spent the last six years building software for a living. For most of that time, one rule held: if a company bought a big piece of business software, it was stuck with it, because rebuilding that software would cost far more than just paying for it year after year. This paper is about why that rule is breaking. With an AI coding assistant that costs a few hundred dollars a month, tools that can take apart almost any program, and open models you can run on your own hardware, a single developer can now rebuild the part of a product that a business actually uses, in weeks instead of years. I walk through three real cases from October 2026, the plain economics behind them, what the law does and does not protect, why hiding how your systems work no longer keeps them safe, and who ends up controlling these tools. I am not saying software has lost its value. I am saying the industry can no longer charge people for how hard it is to leave, and the change is going to be messy.&lt;\/span&gt;&lt;\/p&gt;<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7601279","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,14]],"date-time":"2026-10-14T03:57:28Z","timestamp":1791950248000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3793653.3793788","title":["Towards an Approach for Specifying Intelligent Systems Involving Foundation Model Based Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-8787-8062","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"J\u00falia Cond\u00e9","family":"Ara\u00fajo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Informatics Dept, Pontifical Catholic University of Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-3374-898X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marina Cond\u00e9","family":"Ara\u00fajo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Informatics Dept, Pontifical Catholic University of Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4456-9050","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jos\u00e9 M. C.","family":"Boaro","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Informatics Dept, Pontifical Catholic University of Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1445-3425","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marcos","family":"Kalinowski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Informatics Dept, Pontifical Catholic University of Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Rio de Janeiro, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3793653.3793788","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T16:41:57Z","timestamp":1789576917000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3793653.3793788","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7415278","title":["Measuring Context Assembly Cost in Coding-Agent Trajectories, and Why Software Structure Cannot Be Identified From Them"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-2669-8127","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"YeoHoon","family":"Yoon","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"abstract":"<jats:p>Context: Software modularity principles were designed to reduce the cognitive cost of human maintainers. Coding agents built on large language models (LLMs) now perform a growing share of that work under a different cost structure: every file consulted enters a finite context window, cross-module references are rediscovered through explicit tool calls, and task context is reconstructed rather than remembered. Practitioner discourse holds that co-locating code helps agents, but the evidence is anecdotal and the published evidence on agent-facing documentation points in opposite directions depending on whether a study observed or manipulated it. Objectives: We ask what can be learned about the relationship between software structure and agent behaviour from trajectory data alone, and what cannot. Specifically we operationalise the cost an agent pays to assemble the context a task requires, establish which of its components behave as measurement instruments should, and determine whether the structural factors of interest are identifiable without an experiment. Methods: We instrument a corpus of 554 agent transcripts recorded over 96 days on a production repository, comprising 339 main sessions that resolve to 338 execution lineages and 215 delegated subagent trajectories. From it we derive a multi-dimensional construct we call Context Assembly Cost (CAC), reporting every navigation variable under two path-resolution regimes. Associations are tested with Spearman correlations and Mann-Whitney comparisons under Benjamini-Hochberg correction over a pre&amp;nbsp;specified family; the observational association between documentation access and cost is probed with regression control, nearest-neighbour matching, and a dose-response analysis. Results: Assembly scope is robustly associated with delay to first edit, at rho 0.45 with tool-derived paths alone and 0.65 once shell-issued accesses are resolved, both surviving correction; normalised crossing density reaches no corrected significance under the conservative regime and reverses sign under the broader one. Delegation appears to cut exploration cost per edit 6.1-fold in the parent trajectory, but the delegated trajectories run at 19.4 exploration calls per edit against 0.9 in parents and 77.7% of them make no edit at all. Lineages that consulted documentation cost 1.8 to 2.6 times more per edit; the association survives regression and matching control for observed scope yet does not grow with the amount read. A single session resumption re-emitted 17.4% of all tool-use records at sub-second intervals. In the corpus the three structural factors admit no usable contrast. Conclusion: Trajectory data can establish what to measure and how, and can show that a proposed cause is not identifiable from observation; it cannot estimate the effect of software structure on agents. We report three measurement hazards that any agent-telemetry study must handle, and specify the randomised design that the failure of observational identification makes necessary.<\/jats:p>","URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7415278","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T10:09:43Z","timestamp":1789121383000}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ms.2026.3696850\/mm1","title":["Adopting Large Language Model Agents in Software Testing: A User-Centered Framework_supp1-3696850.pdf"],"author":[{"given":"Billy","family":"Shi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ms.2026.3696850\/mm1","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T19:04:39Z","timestamp":1788807879000},"relation":{"is-component-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.1109\/MS.2026.3696850","asserted-by":"object"}]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3744916.3787782","title":["An empirical Analysis of Community and Coding Patterns in OSS4SG vs. Conventional OSS"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-5316-815X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohamed","family":"Ouf","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen\u2019s University, Kingston, Ontario, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5675-7817","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shayan","family":"Noei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen's University, Kingston, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-7420-6527","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zeph","family":"Van Iterson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen's University, Kingston, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2545-2612","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mariam","family":"Guizani","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen's University, Kingston, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5335-0261","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ying","family":"Zou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Queen\u2019s University, Kingston, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3744916.3787782","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T12:11:14Z","timestamp":1789128674000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3744916.3787782","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) have advanced autonomous agents\u2019 planning and decision-making, yet they struggle with complex tasks requiring diverse expertise and multi-step reasoning. Multi-Agent Debate (MAD) systems, introduced in NLP research, address this gap by enabling structured debates among LLM-based agents to refine solutions iteratively. MAD promotes divergent thinking through role-specific agents, dynamic interactions, and structured decision-making. Recognizing parallels between Software Engineering (SE) and collaborative human problem-solving, this study investigates MAD's effectiveness on four coding tasks in SE. We adapt a MAD framework from NLP, analyze agent interactions to assess consensus-building and iterative refinement, and propose two MAD variants that enhance agent debate for coding tasks by addressing the observed weaknesses. Our findings show that structured debate and collaboration improve problem-solving and yield strong performance in some cases, highlighting the collaborative debate synergy between the LLM-agents for coding tasks in SE while identifying areas for exploration.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3846189","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,10]],"date-time":"2026-09-10T19:40:25Z","timestamp":1789069225000},"title":["Enhancing LLM Performance Through Debate: An Empirical Study on Multi-Agent Debate for Coding Tasks"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-4982-7786","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yong Jin","family":"Chun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Irvine, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-4844-4717","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"QiHong","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Irvine, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4434-4812","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jiawei","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Irvine, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8221-5352","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Iftekhar","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of California, Irvine, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3846189","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3846189","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,10]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Effectively testing Artificial Intelligence (AI) agents remains a fundamental challenge due to their stochastic reasoning, vast and diverse input space, reliance on external tools, and operation in dynamic execution environments; factors that demand new testing methodologies explicitly tailored to the complex and interactive nature of agent-based systems. This work presents a novel methodology for testing AI agents, with a particular focus on assessing their behavioral robustness under varied operational conditions. Our approach relies on following key technical innovations: (1) a coverage-guided test input generation strategy based on agent- specific coverage objectives, (2) a capture-and-simulate mechanism that systematically emulates abnormal tool behaviors to mimic real-world execution failures, and (3) a deterministic behavioral failure detection approach that enables consistent identification of failures across different test inputs. We developed AgentInspect, a framework that automatically detects six types of behavioral failures in LangChain-based AI agents by analyzing their execution trajectories across three evaluation settings: a baseline setting using real tool responses, a simulated setting incorporating synthetic tool responses, and a hybrid setting that combines the real and simulated tool responses. To evaluate our approach, we curated a benchmark of 35 AI agents obtained from GitHub. Our results show that AgentInspect consistently identifies different behavioral failures with high precision and recall across all three execution settings. In particular, the simulated and hybrid settings expose failure modes that do not emerge during baseline execution with real tool responses, thereby enabling a more comprehensive assessment of agent robustness. Our findings highlight AgentInspect\u2019s effectiveness in revealing critical failures and its practical utility for systematic robustness evaluation of AI agents.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3832174","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T19:32:02Z","timestamp":1790883122000},"title":["AgentInspect: Diagnosing Behavioral Failures in Artificial Intelligence Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-4729-8421","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ruchira","family":"Manke","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tulane University, Dept. of Computer Science, New Orleans, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-0213-725X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad","family":"Wardat","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Oakland University, Dept. of Computer Science and Engineering, Rochester, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5704-4173","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Foutse","family":"Khomh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Polytechnique Montreal, SWAT Lab., Montr\u00e9al, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9410-9562","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hridesh","family":"Rajan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tulane University, Dept. of Computer Science, New Orleans, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832174","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"syndication"},{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832174","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832174","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3829641.3829782","title":["A Coding-Settlement Coupling Intelligent Schedule Control Method for Mass Pile Foundations in Ultra-Deep Soft Foundation Seawalls"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-5197-8064","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Fan","family":"Wu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Power China Huadong Engineering Corporation Limited, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-0743-3454","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pan","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Power China Huadong Engineering Corporation Limited, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-4926-2959","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Delian","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Power China Huadong Engineering Corporation Limited, Hangzhou, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3829641.3829782","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,8]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T06:42:51Z","timestamp":1789540971000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3829641.3829782","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834427","title":["Barista: Synthesizing Typestate Specifications with LLM Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6995-7340","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Catarina","family":"Gamboa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, USA"},{"name":"University of Lisbon, Lasige, Lisbon, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0154-8989","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Paulo","family":"Canelas","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, USA"},{"name":"University of Lisbon, Lasige, Lisbon, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-5674-4200","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ricardo","family":"Costa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Lisbon, Lasige, Lisbon, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-3815-2113","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"M\u00e1rcio","family":"Caetano","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Lisbon, Lasige, Lisbon, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0631-5591","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jonathan","family":"Aldrich","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University, Pittsburgh, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0879-4015","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Alcides","family":"Fonseca","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Lisbon, Lasige, Lisbon, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834427","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834427","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3744916.3773269","title":["LLM-based Agents for Automated Bug Fixing: How Far Are We?"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5630-4810","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiangxin","family":"Meng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-2846-7399","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zexiong","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3800-2565","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pengfei","family":"Gao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2843-0689","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chao","family":"Peng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ByteDance, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3744916.3773269","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T12:11:14Z","timestamp":1789128674000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3744916.3773269","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3832783.3834413","title":["Understanding the Code and Architectural Smells Generated by LLMs and Autonomous Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0978-8389","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuecai","family":"Zhu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Concordia University, Montreal, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2508-3845","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nikolaos","family":"Tsantalis","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Concordia University, Montreal, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1137-4297","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Peter C.","family":"Rigby","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Concordia University, Montreal, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832783.3834413","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,7]],"date-time":"2026-10-07T22:18:46Z","timestamp":1791411526000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832783.3834413","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3744916.3787835","title":["TACO: Trust Assessment of Large Language Models in Coding Assistance Tasks"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-8817-8723","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shihao","family":"Weng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University, Nanjing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-7477-3642","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yang","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University, Nanjing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-1780-2030","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jincheng","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University, Nanjing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-1574-2758","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yining","family":"Yin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University, Nanjing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1052-0053","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhenlun","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University, Nanjing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-4105-9158","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lyuxi","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Virginia, Virginia, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8368-4898","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jia","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Key Laboratory for Novel Software Technology, Nanjing University, Nanjing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3744916.3787835","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,11]],"date-time":"2026-09-11T12:11:14Z","timestamp":1789128674000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3744916.3787835","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3793657.3793886","title":["Software Engineering as Deep Research"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4147-3942","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Klaus","family":"Schmid","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Computer Science \/ Software Systems Engineering, University of Hildesheim, Hildesheim, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3793657.3793886","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,14]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T16:06:38Z","timestamp":1791821198000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3793657.3793886","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.14711\/thesis-hdl174867","title":["Adaptive Security for Complex Software Systems: Runtime Decision Making in Fuzzing and LLM Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Hung-chun","family":"Chiu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14711\/thesis-hdl174867","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,17]],"date-time":"2026-09-17T23:14:30Z","timestamp":1789686870000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3774748.3787604","title":["Automated Testing of Conversational Agents with Chatbot D\u014dj\u014d"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-6295-7007","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Iv\u00e1n","family":"Sotillo del Horno","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science Department, Universidad Autonoma de Madrid, Madrid, Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-3386-162X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Alejandro","family":"del Pozzo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science Department, Universidad Autonoma de Madrid, Madrid, Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2818-2278","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Esther","family":"Guerra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science Department, Universidad Autonoma de Madrid, Madrid, Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9425-6362","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Juan","family":"de Lara","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Computer Science Department, Universidad Autonoma de Madrid, Madrid, Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3774748.3787604","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,9]],"date-time":"2026-09-09T21:41:26Z","timestamp":1788990086000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3774748.3787604","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Wilayah pesisir Padang Pariaman merupakan kawasan rawan gempa akibat aktivitas tektonik subduksi lempeng. Penelitian ini bertujuan merancang prototipe sistem peringatan dini gempa bumi berbasis Arduino Uno dengan sensor getar SW-420 yang terjangkau bagi masyarakat pesisir. Metode yang digunakan meliputi perancangan\u00a0hardware\u00a0(sensor SW-420, Arduino Uno, LED indikator,\u00a0buzzer, LCD I2C) dan\u00a0software\u00a0dengan algoritma\u00a0pulse counting\u00a0dalam\u00a0window time\u00a0300 ms. Sistem mengklasifikasikan getaran menjadi tiga status: normal (LED hijau berkedip), getaran ringan (LED kuning +\u00a0buzzer\u00a0900 Hz), dan gempa besar (LED merah +\u00a0buzzer\u00a01500\/700 Hz) dengan tampilan status pada LCD. Hasil pengujian menunjukkan sistem mampu membedakan intensitas getaran dengan akurasi 90-100% dan waktu respons 1.7-1.9 detik. Prototipe ini berpotensi sebagai solusi\u00a0low-cost\u00a0untuk meningkatkan kesiapsiagaan bencana gempa di tingkat komunitas pesisir.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5057","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:52Z","timestamp":1792035172000},"title":["Prototipe Sistem Deteksi Getaran dan Alarm Gempa Berbasis Arduino Uno Menggunakan Sensor SW-420 untuk Wilayah Pesisir Padang Pariaman"],"author":[{"given":"Hellia","family":"Marsanti","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Edwar","family":"Rosman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Katrina","family":"Flomina G","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fathurrahman","family":"Kurniawan Ikhsan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5057\/2840","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/5057\/2840","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v2i1.5057","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]}},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Penelitian ini bertujuan untuk menganalisis pengaruh persepsi kemanfaatan, persepsi kemudahan penggunaan, dan persepsi risiko terhadap penerimaan (acceptance) Quick Response Code Indonesian Standard (QRIS) sebagai alat pembayaran digital di kalangan mahasiswa Universitas Muhammadiyah Riau (UMRI). Penelitian ini menggunakan pendekatan kuantitatif dengan menyebar kuesioner kepada 50 mahasiswa. Teknik analisis data yang digunakan adalah analisis regresi linear berganda setelah melalui uji kualitas data (validitas dan reliabilitas) dan uji asumsi klasik (normalitas, multikolinearitas, dan heteroskedastisitas). Hasil penelitian menunjukkan bahwa: (1) Persepsi kemanfaatan berpengaruh positif dan signifikan terhadap penerimaan QRIS. (2) Persepsi kemudahan penggunaan berpengaruh positif dan signifikan terhadap penerimaan QRIS. (3) Persepsi risiko berpengaruh negatif dan signifikan terhadap penerimaan QRIS. Hasil uji asumsi klasik menunjukkan model regresi memenuhi semua kriteria yang disyaratkan. Koefisien determinasi (Adjusted R-Square) menunjukkan bahwa 53.5% variasi penerimaan QRIS dapat dijelaskan oleh ketiga variabel tersebut.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.25181\/coding.v2i1.4273","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,15]],"date-time":"2026-10-15T03:32:52Z","timestamp":1792035172000},"title":["Analisis Persepsi Kemanfaatan, Kemudahan, dan Risiko terhadap Penerimaan QRIS sebagai Alat Pembayaran Digital oleh Mahasiswa Universitas Muhammadiyah Riau"],"author":[{"given":"Fauzan","family":"Azim","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rayhan","family":"Aulia Rahman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dwi","family":"Febri Riyanto","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dewi","family":"Liri Linora","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Irfan","family":"Ramadan Pratama","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4273\/2842","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/jurnal.polinela.ac.id\/index.php\/coding\/article\/download\/4273\/2842","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.25181\/coding.v2i1.4273","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]}},{"DOI":"10.1016\/s0965-9978(26)00214-0","title":["Editorial Board"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/s0965-9978(26)00214-0","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,11]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,7]],"date-time":"2026-09-07T06:38:03Z","timestamp":1788763083000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0965997826002140?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0965997826002140?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"DOI":"10.70593\/978-81-6632-146-7","title":["Code You Didn't Write, Risk You Still Own: Governing LLM Coding Assistants in Regulated Financial Software - Second Edition"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-8578-9321","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ashutosh","family":"Pal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70593\/978-81-6632-146-7","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,13]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,18]],"date-time":"2026-10-18T09:26:46Z","timestamp":1792315606000}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3793653.3793774","title":["Engineering AI Agents for Clinical Workflows: A Case Study in Architecture, MLOps, and Governance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1655-2283","authenticated-orcid":false,"suffix":"Dr.","given":"Claudio Lucio Do Val","family":"Lopes","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"PPMMC, A3Data, CEFET-MG, Belo Horizonte, MG, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-6750-6478","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jo\u00e3o Marcus","family":"Pitta","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"A3Data, BH, MG, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1076-2052","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Fabiano Muniz","family":"Bel\u00e9m","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"A3Data, BH, MG, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-7490-7365","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Gildson","family":"Alves","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"A3Data, Belo Horizonte, MG, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6666-653X","authenticated-orcid":false,"suffix":"Dr.","given":"Fl\u00e1vio Vin\u00edcius Cruzeiro","family":"Martins","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"DECOM, CEFET-MG, Belo Horizonte, MG, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3793653.3793774","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,16]],"date-time":"2026-09-16T16:41:57Z","timestamp":1789576917000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3793653.3793774","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.advengsoft.2026.104337","title":["Editorial Board"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.advengsoft.2026.104337","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,23]],"date-time":"2026-10-23T02:54:52Z","timestamp":1792724092000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0965997826002425?httpAccept=text\/xml","content-type":"text\/xml","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/api.elsevier.com\/content\/article\/PII:S0965997826002425?httpAccept=text\/plain","content-type":"text\/plain","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Graphical User Interface (GUI) agents are increasingly deployed to interact with online web services, yet their exposure to open-world content renders them vulnerable to Environmental Injection Attacks (EIAs). In these attacks, an attacker can inject crafted triggers into a website to manipulate the behavior of other users\u2019 GUI agents. In this paper, we find that most existing EIA studies fall short of realism. In particular, they fail to capture the dynamic nature of real-world websites, often assuming that a trigger\u2019s on-screen position and surrounding visual context remain largely consistent between training and testing. To better reflect practice, we introduce a realistic dynamic-environment threat model in which the attacker is a regular user and the trigger is embedded within a dynamically changing environment. Under this threat model, existing approaches largely fail, suggesting that their effectiveness in exposing GUI agent vulnerabilities has been overestimated.<\/jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>To expose the hidden vulnerabilities of existing GUI agents effectively, we propose Chameleon, an attack framework with two key components designed for dynamic environments. (1) To synthesize more realistic training data, we introduce LLM-Driven Environment Simulation, which automatically generates diverse, high-fidelity webpage simulations that mimic the variability of real-world dynamic environments. (2) To optimize the trigger more effectively, we introduce Attention Black Hole, which converts attention weights into explicit supervisory signals. We evaluate Chameleon on six realistic websites and four representative LVLM-powered GUI agents. Across these settings, it significantly outperforms existing methods. Ablation studies confirm that both components are critical to performance, and a closed-loop sandbox experiment further demonstrates that Chameleon can successfully hijack agent behavior in conditions that closely mirror real-world usage. Our results uncover a critical, previously underexplored vulnerability of GUI agents in realistic dynamic environments and establish a robust foundation for future research on defenses for open-world GUI agent systems.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3832148","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T19:32:02Z","timestamp":1790883122000},"title":["Environmental Injection Attacks against GUI Agents in Realistic Dynamic Environments"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-1138-4503","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yitong","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Tsinghua University, College of AI, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-6594-9897","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ximo","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tsinghua University, Department of Computer Science and Technology, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-7848-6007","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Liyi","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, School of Computer Science, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5579-8852","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jia","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tsinghua University, College of AI, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832148","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832148","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]}},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3793657.3793876","title":["Ethics of Care for Software Engineering"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1418-0095","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Alexander","family":"Serebrenik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Eindhoven University of Technology, Eindhoven, Netherlands"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2442-7522","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sebastian","family":"Baltes","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Heidelberg University, Heidelberg, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3793657.3793876","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,14]]},"created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,12]],"date-time":"2026-10-12T16:06:38Z","timestamp":1791821198000},"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3793657.3793876","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}]},{"abstract":"<jats:p>Static Application Security Testing (SAST) tools are essential for identifying software vulnerabilities, but they often produce a high volume of False Positives (FPs), imposing a substantial manual triage burden on developers. Recent advances in Large Language Model (LLM) agents offer a promising direction by enabling iterative reasoning, tool use, and environment interaction to refine SAST alerts. However, the comparative effectiveness of different LLM-based agent architectures for FP filtering remains poorly understood.<\/jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>In this paper, we present a comparative study of three state-of-the-art LLM-based agent frameworks, i.e., Aider, OpenHands, and SWE-agent, for vulnerability FP filtering. We evaluate these frameworks using the vulnerabilities from the OWASP Benchmark and real-world open-source Java projects. We further conduct a focused post-cutoff C\/C++ study using the strongest configuration to test contamination-free generalization and isolate key agentic capabilities. The experimental results show that LLM-based agents can remove the majority of SAST noise, reducing an initial FP detection rate of over 92% on the OWASP Benchmark to as low as 6.3% in the best configuration. On a real-world Java dataset, the best configuration of LLM-based agents can achieve an FP identification rate of up to 93.3% involving CodeQL alerts. However, the benefits of agents are strongly backbone- and CWE-dependent: agentic frameworks significantly outperform vanilla prompting for stronger models such as Claude Sonnet 4 and GPT-5, but yield limited or inconsistent gains for weaker backbones. On the post-cutoff OSS-Fuzz dataset, SWE-agent with Claude Sonnet 4 identifies 95.5% of FPs while maintaining 95.5% precision, compared with a 36.4% FP identification rate for vanilla prompting. Moreover, aggressive FP reduction can come at the cost of suppressing true vulnerabilities, highlighting important trade-offs. Finally, we observe large disparities in computational cost across agent frameworks.<\/jats:p>\n                  <jats:p>Overall, our study demonstrates that LLM-based agents are a powerful but non-uniform solution for SAST FP filtering, and that their practical deployment requires careful consideration of agent design, backbone model choice, vulnerability category, and operational cost.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1145\/3832100","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]],"date-time":"2026-10-01T19:32:02Z","timestamp":1790883122000},"title":["Sifting the Noise: A Comparative Study of LLM Agents in Vulnerability False Positive Filtering"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-4330-4549","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yunpeng","family":"Xiong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Monash University, Melbourne, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6001-1372","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ting","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Monash University, Melbourne, Australia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/dl.acm.org\/doi\/pdf\/10.1145\/3832100","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3832100","published":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,1]]}}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}